        release.sh,
        setup.cfg,
        scripts/check_changelog.py,
        .qgis-plugin-ci,
        .github/workflows/release.yml,
      ]
    message: >
      Three ADVERTISED features were dead code paths, each with a passing
      test suite that used fixtures too extreme or too gated to expose them.
      (1) CSF Ground Filter raised TypeError on every LAS->DEM run:
      gdal.GridOptions takes zfield as a field NAME and got the column index
      2, and gdal.Grid was fed a .xyz file, which OGR cannot open as a vector
      source. Nothing covered filter_las_file at all. (2) The CuPy GPU
      backend was called from nowhere in the plugin, and held three defects —
      _shift_array_gpu computed the overlap as `size` or `size - 2*|shift|`
      instead of `size - |shift|`, so every horizon step raised a broadcast
      ValueError; round(cos)/round(sin) quantised every azimuth to one of 8
      integer directions, silently collapsing 16 and 32; and
      cp.is_available() raises past an except-ImportError guard on a broken
      driver, taking the plugin down at QGIS start-up. (3) Local Dominance
      returned radians then byte-scaled with degree-scale limits
      ((v-0.5)/1.3*255), so every pixel clipped to zero on anything gentler
      than a cliff — found by rendering the new contact sheet, where its
      panel was blank while ten others showed the test earthworks.
      Also fixed: per-tile normalisation in ML inference (a uniform tile
      scaled to all zeros regardless of elevation, wrecking segmentation
      mosaics); process_in_tiles writing untagged NaN when the source
      declared no nodata; GDAL error branches unreachable under
      gdal.UseExceptions(); dem_difference's dead ["crs","transform"] loop
      calling deprecated rio.set_crs(); and an SVF docstring that wrongly
      claimed the implementation deviates from Zaksek et al. 2011 (verified
      against rvt-py — the linear form IS the reference).
      Added: Visualisation Contact Sheet, radii in metres (presets held
      pixels, so they were only valid on a 1 m DEM), semantic segmentation
      for AI detection, and provenance sidecars + an inspect algorithm.
      MEASURE BEFORE OPTIMISING HERE. np.hypot was 1.54s of 2.00s of array
      arithmetic (7x slower than sqrt(dz*dz+d*d), which agrees to float32
      epsilon); my first guess, allocation in _shift_array, was 2.5%.
      Tiled work is MEMORY-BANDWIDTH bound: parallelism caps at 1.7x and
      four workers measured SLOWER than two on some raster shapes, with
      processes no better than threads. Do not size the pool from cpu_count.
      Threads also accept the local closures several wrappers pass, which
      ProcessPoolExecutor cannot pickle.
      Left open: metadata.txt still reads 2.0.22 and needs a bump before
      tagging (the release guard fails on a mismatch); nine untracked
      pre-2.0 ZIPs remain in _archive/ as the only surviving copies of
      versions with neither a git tag nor a GitHub release; PDAL Python
      bindings and CUDA are absent locally so those tests still skip.
      Tests 207 -> 387.

# Dependency policy: core terrain tools must work with what QGIS bundles.
# Every optional package is import-guarded and degrades with an install
# hint — see check_dependencies() in the modules that need one. Adding a
# hard dependency outside `required` is a breaking change for users who
# install from the QGIS Plugin Manager.
packages:
  python:
    required:
      # Supplied by QGIS itself; the plugin never pins these.
      - gdal # osgeo.gdal / osgeo.ogr / osgeo.osr — all raster and vector I/O
      - numpy>=1.20 # every core algorithm
      - qgis # PyQGIS, used only by plugin.py, provider.py and algorithms/
    optional:
      - scipy # SLRM uniform_filter; falls back to a NumPy box filter
      - rvt-py # RVT reference implementations + the golden-regression suite
      - rio-cogeo # COG export
      - reportlab # PDF reports
      - cloth-simulation-filter # CSF ground filter (imports as CSF)
      - laspy # LAS/LAZ reading; PDAL is the alternative
      - pdal # PDAL classification pipelines
      - xarray # multi-temporal DEM difference
      - rioxarray # multi-temporal + fusion
      - rasterio # multi-sensor Sentinel-2 fusion
      - onnxruntime # AI feature detection and segmentation inference
      - opencv-python # faster tile resize in ML preprocessing; NumPy fallback exists
      - Pillow # contact sheet panel captions; sheet still renders unlabelled without it
      - cupy-cuda12x # GPU backend for SVF and Openness; NumPy fallback always available
    dev:
      - pytest
      - ruff
      - flake8
      - onnx # builds the synthetic segmentation model in test_ml_segmentation.py
